dpeg wrote:
> I have been thinking about this a bit more, it bugs me a lot.
> 
> Crawl opening up has caused the following phenomena:
> * players writing patches and bugfixes
> * players contributing new vaults (predefined maps that need to come in 
>   as large a variety as possible for good effect)
> * players discussing changes in-depth, hinting at balance issues

Each of the points can be applied to Nethack, too. We already have
patches, bugfixes, contributions, and discussions. What we don't have
are *public* discussions about design issues _with the Devteam_.
Do we need that? (The Devteam apparently doesn't seem to aim at that.)

> Each of these has problems: we may not like a certain patch (and will not
> use it then). A new vault may be buggy or way too hard (dito). Players
> confuse "oh my, this godly invocation did kill me - bug! balance broken!!
> murder and treason!!!" with proper feedback.

Not any single detection of newly introduced imbalances is the primary
problem. I'd suspect that in consequence there are a lot of releases
in short term; the product will continuously change without getting
to a stable point with tuned features which would allow for a reliable
play. Personally I wouldn't like to play a game that will change every,
say, three (or so) months. YMMV.

One can imagine that it might evolve better, though; development of
test releases, off from the product delivery branch, and occasionally
a merge to a new product release. But who will decide which feature to
be incorporated, which general design route to be taken? "Development
by Committee"[tm]?

> All of these could be muted down by moving into the Ivory Tower. But: we
> gain a lot more than what we expend in resources

What exactly do you think do we gain?

Either way we already have enough coders for contributions. But, more
essentially, do we have too few software design architects for Nethack?
(I don't know about your profession and your project management experience
in software development, therefore I ask you this quite innocently.)

